---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/case/CaseStudy.astro';
import BookingFlow from '../../components/case/BookingFlow.astro';
import { projectRecords } from '../../data/portfolio';
import { sessionbookMeta, sessionbookBoundary } from '../../data/sessionbook';

const title = 'SessionBook — Booking backend for a voice agent | Stephen Mensah';
const description = projectRecords.sessionbook.summary;
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CreativeWork',
  name: 'SessionBook',
  description,
  url: new URL('/work/sessionbook/', Astro.site).href,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Stephen Mensah' },
  about: { '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode', codeRepository: sessionbookMeta.repoUrl },
};
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} {structuredData} ogType="article">
  <CaseStudy id="sessionbook">
    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <BookingFlow />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="problem">
      <p>
        A voice agent can ask a caller what time works. It shouldn’t decide whether a slot exists,
        whether someone else just booked it, or whether a confirmation is valid.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the design problem in one line: the conversation is open, the booking is a
        transaction. Everything in this service follows from keeping those two things apart.
      </p>

      <div class="compare-grid">
        <div class="compare-sheet">
          <h3>The agent owns</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Transcription and turn-taking</li>
            <li>Prompting and the decision to call a tool</li>
            <li>Speech — how the answer is said</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-sheet compare-sheet--after">
          <h3>The backend owns</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Which slots are actually free</li>
            <li>Reserving one without double-booking</li>
            <li>Timezones, confirmation codes, what to say when it fails</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="contribution">
      <p>
        I built the FastAPI service behind that boundary: the HTTP tools the agent calls,
        availability queries, the transaction around booking, and the responses designed to be read
        aloud.
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Four HTTP tools</strong> — <code>get_today</code>, <code>check_availability</code>,
          <code>book_slot</code> and <code>confirm_booking</code>, mounted as
          <code>POST /tools</code> routes the agent calls itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The booking service</strong> — <code>app/services/booking_service.py</code>:
          availability, the locked booking transaction, confirmation codes and spoken labels.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The contracts</strong> — <code>schemas.py</code>: availability and booking
          requests with length-bounded fields and service-level phone validation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The agent configuration</strong> — <code>voice_agent/agent.json</code>: four tool
          definitions pointing at those routes.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="case-note">
        AssemblyAI owns the conversation. SessionBook owns the agent document and the backend the
        agent calls — nothing here has to stream or persist a WebSocket.
      </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="decisions">
      <dl class="decision-list">
        <div>
          <dt>Lock the slot before checking it</dt>
          <dd>
            <code>book_slot</code> takes <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> inside a transaction, and
            only then checks that the slot exists and is still free. The existence check happens
            after the lock is held, so two concurrent callers cannot both pass the gate.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Let the database be the final arbiter</dt>
          <dd>
            A unique slot constraint plus an <code>IntegrityError</code> fallback means the second
            writer fails loudly instead of double-booking; the service raises
            <code>SlotAlreadyBookedError</code>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Keep the clock provider-local</dt>
          <dd>
            Datetimes are timezone-aware and rendered for speech in Africa/Accra through
            <code>ZoneInfo</code> — the provider’s clock, not the caller’s.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Make the confirmation sayable</dt>
          <dd>
            Confirmation codes run six to ten characters with no 0/O or 1/I, and arrive with a
            <code>spoken_confirmation</code> sentence so a caller can read the code back.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Give failures something to say</dt>
          <dd>
            The tool routes return speakable sentences — 409 “That slot was just taken, would you
            like another time?” and 404 for a slot that does not exist — so the agent can ask the
            next question instead of apologising.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="result">
      <p>{projectRecords.sessionbook.result}</p>

      <dl class="case-states">
        <div>
          <dt>Implementation</dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>In source at commit 1264d9e.</strong> Four mounted tool routes, a row-locking
            booking service, the Pydantic contracts and the agent configuration.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Live calls</dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>Unverified.</strong> No call was made, retained or replayed, and no deployment
            is verified.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="case-sub">Present in source</p>
      <ul class="case-evidence case-evidence--known">
        {sessionbookBoundary.known.slice(0, 6).map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="limits">
      <p>
        Repository inspection verifies written implementation, not a working phone call. The
        boundary between what exists and what has been proven is the whole point of this page, so
        it stays stated rather than implied.
      </p>

      <ul class="case-evidence case-evidence--missing">
        {sessionbookBoundary.missing.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <p class="case-note">
        Two corrections worth keeping: the earlier portfolio claim that short phone numbers were
        still accepted was stale — the service validates normalised numbers at 7–15 digits — and
        tool authentication has not been reviewed. The test file is empty and no Alembic migration
        version has been generated.
      </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="sources">
      <ul class="case-sources">
        <li>
          <a href={sessionbookMeta.repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source repository</a>
          <span class="case-sources__tag">GitHub</span>
          <span>FastAPI service, agent configuration and setup notes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={`${sessionbookMeta.repoUrl}/tree/${sessionbookMeta.repoCommit}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Source at {sessionbookMeta.repoCommit}
          </a>
          <span class="case-sources__tag">{sessionbookMeta.repoCommitDate}</span>
          <span>The commit this page was read against.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={sessionbookMeta.tutorialUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            AssemblyAI HTTP tools tutorial
          </a>
          <span class="case-sources__tag">Reference</span>
          <span>
            Conceptual reference for the shape of the integration. Nothing here is claimed as an
            implemented SessionBook feature.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="case-note">
        There is no live demo to link to. The README’s setup instructions and an agent ID file do
        not establish a deployment, and no call recording or transcript exists.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseStudy>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .compare-sheet h3 {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-family: var(--font-instrument);
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-on-paper-faint);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }
  .compare-sheet ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
